{:mulog/duration 350708, :mulog/namespace "ai.obney.grain.command-processor-v2.core", :mulog/outcome :ok, :mulog/parent-trace nil, :ai.obney.grain.command-processor-v2.core/command {:tick-id #uuid "949f7490-3e63-4ab2-a805-026ed32efc9b", :node-path [{:type :leaf, :node-id #uuid "3fe0c114-3d94-4ed5-bea2-589c6ff6de48"}], :command/name :sheet/record-rlm-tree-node-completion, :command/timestamp #time/offset-date-time "2026-05-19T20:56:28.010827-05:00", :command/id #uuid "9a08d8df-adc0-4721-af9a-7b320684c364", :usage {:prompt-tokens 1512, :completion-tokens 640, :total-tokens 2152}, :node-id #uuid "3fe0c114-3d94-4ed5-bea2-589c6ff6de48", :input-profile {:section-diffs {:type :string, :length 3024, :word-count 428, :line-count 28}, :major-changes {:type :string, :length 2473, :word-count 341, :line-count 21}}, :sheet-id #uuid "74303876-0ee3-4ad2-9076-81cf7a129476"}, :mulog/root-trace #mulog/flake "5A3YKxcITY8X_VZ3bpfLaPTK1ZBTbvQv", :mulog/timestamp 1779242188010, :metric/name "CommandProcessed", :mulog/trace-id #mulog/flake "5A3YKxcITY8X_VZ3bpfLaPTK1ZBTbvQv", :mulog/event-name :ai.obney.grain.command-processor-v2.core/process-command, :metric/resolution :high}
{:mulog/parent-trace #mulog/flake "5A3YKxcNKzSXT2k3CM4wQ-4SWAWf5T20", :mulog/root-trace #mulog/flake "5A3YKxcNKzSXT2k3CM4wQ-4SWAWf5T20", :mulog/trace-id #mulog/flake "5A3YKxcNvrteLpyIE8sECg_ai8IeUkqF", :mulog/timestamp 1779242188011, :mulog/event-name :ai.obney.grain.todo-processor-v2.core/anomaly-in-todo-processor, :mulog/namespace "ai.obney.grain.todo-processor-v2.core", :anomaly {:cognitect.anomalies/category :cognitect.anomalies/fault, :cognitect.anomalies/message "Todo Processor returned nil: %s"}}
{:mulog/duration 42208, :mulog/namespace "ai.obney.grain.todo-processor-v2.core", :mulog/outcome :ok, :mulog/parent-trace nil, :event {:tick-id #uuid "949f7490-3e63-4ab2-a805-026ed32efc9b", :writes {:impact-analysis "Based on the textual differences between Version 2.0 and Version 3.1.1, the impact analysis is as follows:\n\n### 1. Shift in Risk Profile\n*   **Supplier Risk Reduced:** The primary risk shift is a significant reduction in compliance and default risk for the **Supplier**. The removal of the 60% Domestic Content Requirement eliminates a major operational hurdle, procurement constraint, and potential point of contractual breach. Suppliers are no longer tethered to local supply chain availability or the administrative burden of documenting and proving the origin of components.\n*   **OPA Administrative Control Increased:** While the OPA loses the ability to enforce domestic content targets, it gains tighter administrative control through the \"Prescribed Form\" requirements and \"sole and absolute discretion\" clauses. The risk to the OPA is a potential loss of local economic development benefits, but this is offset by reduced litigation/oversight risk regarding domestic content audits.\n\n### 2. Party Favorability\n*   **Strongly Favors the Supplier:** \n    *   **Elimination of Appendix C:** The removal of the complex Domestic Content Grids and the 60% threshold is a massive win for Suppliers. It allows for global sourcing of components (likely at lower costs) without risking contract eligibility.\n    *   **Eligibility Flexibility:** The modifications to \"Project Eligibility Requirements\" and the \"Existing Building\" definition (Appendix A & C) allow the OPA to grant exceptions in their \"sole and absolute discretion.\" This provides a pathway for projects that might have been disqualified under the strict rules of Version 2.0 to remain viable through OPA consent.\n*   **Neutral/Administrative for the OPA:**\n    *   **Standardization:** The requirement for \"Prescribed Forms\" (Sections 5, 7, 8, 9) favors the OPA’s internal processing and data management, ensuring that notices of termination or amendment are uniform and legally robust.\n    *   **Public Safety Disclosure:** The new provision in Section 6.5 reduces liability for the OPA when sharing data with emergency services, clarifying their right to act in the interest of public safety.\n\n### 3. Key Legal & Operational Impacts\n*   **Procurement Strategy:** Suppliers can now optimize for price and technology rather than geographic origin.\n*   **Notice Compliance:** The introduction of the \"Prescribed Form\" definition means that informal notices (e.g., a standard letter or email) may no longer be contractually valid for major actions like termination or amendments, requiring stricter adherence to OPA’s web-based templates.\n*   **Confidentiality:** There is a slight narrowing of privacy as the OPA expands its right to disclose facility details to government agencies for emergency planning, though this is a standard regulatory evolution.\n\n**Conclusion:** The transition to Version 3.1.1 represents a **deregulatory shift** that primarily benefits the Supplier by removing protectionist trade requirements and offering more flexible eligibility pathways, while standardizing administrative interactions through prescribed digital forms."}, :duration-ms 4713, :event/tags #{[:node #uuid "3fe0c114-3d94-4ed5-bea2-589c6ff6de48"] [:sheet #uuid "74303876-0ee3-4ad2-9076-81cf7a129476"] [:tick #uuid "949f7490-3e63-4ab2-a805-026ed32efc9b"]}, :event/timestamp #time/offset-date-time "2026-05-19T20:56:28.006881-05:00", :usage {:prompt-tokens 1512, :completion-tokens 640, :total-tokens 2152}, :status :success, :node-id #uuid "3fe0c114-3d94-4ed5-bea2-589c6ff6de48", :event/type :sheet/node-execution-completed, :sheet-id #uuid "74303876-0ee3-4ad2-9076-81cf7a129476", :event/id #uuid "019e4319-3ce6-70a2-8498-9040cb34abf1"}, :mulog/root-trace #mulog/flake "5A3YKxcNKzSXT2k3CM4wQ-